{
  "image_name": "dc54e6a2c0e8fcc73d1a7b43821b96cb.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how coastal groundwater systems connect land, the subsurface, and the nearshore ocean. The main cross-section shows fresh coastal groundwater on the landward side being recharged by precipitation and influenced by evapotranspiration and water-table fluctuations. That freshwater flows seaward and discharges near the coast, while saline coastal groundwater occupies deeper and more marine parts of the aquifer. Between them is a freshwater–saltwater interface and a transition zone, with a subterranean estuary where mixing, chemical gradients, and microbial communities appear to shape nutrient and element cycling.\n\nThe ocean side is linked to the aquifer through several pathways: fresh submarine groundwater discharge, saline submarine groundwater discharge, and saline recirculation driven by tides and wave run-up. These flows connect groundwater chemistry to nearshore ecology and coastal ocean chemistry, emphasizing that coastal groundwater is both hydrologically dynamic and biogeochemically active.\n\nThe lower panels compare two responses to sea-level rise. In a topography-limited setting, rising sea level causes inundation from water-table rise and promotes seawater intrusion. In a recharge-limited setting, the land surface appears able to accommodate some water-table rise, but seawater intrusion still increases as the freshwater lens shifts inland and upward.",
